+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edits — Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+## Context
+
+TidePlot's field workers correct the same plot visit independently, sometimes days apart, on shared tablets that can be offline for 48 hours. The record store receives submissions whose starting state may already be stale, and arrival order carries no authority about whose observations are right. The concurrency mechanism belongs to the record store, and tablet-sync and review-export cannot be designed until it settles: it fixes submission metadata, the shape of coordinator review work, and which visits may enter the Friday CSV.
+
+What is at stake: with too permissive a policy, the server silently combines or discards scientific judgment; with too heavy a policy, two coordinators drown in review work and the export is habitually blocked. Pre-TidePlot spreadsheet imports overwrote corrections without showing which submission was lost — the failure mode this decision exists to prevent.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Gates (binary, inherited from the HLD tenets):
+
+- **No silent discard.** A policy that discards or overwrites a submitted edit without human review is eliminated. This removes last-write-wins and reject-stale-submissions from contention before criteria apply.
+- **Operable by two coordinators.** The policy's day-to-day operation must fit a team with no dedicated systems administrator.
+
+Weighted criteria:
+
+- **Safety of scientifically related edits** — major. Species identifications, cover estimates, and notes relate in ways field names do not encode; whatever decides that two edits may be combined must not outrank the people who understand the science. (Turns on the tenet to keep the observation-to-export path inspectable.)
+- **Coordinator review load** — major. Roughly 6,000 visits per season, of which some fraction carry concurrent edits; the queue must be resolvable by two people before the Friday export.
+- **Implementation and maintenance burden** — moderate. Merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries are ongoing code that someone must maintain and trust.
+- **Explainability to field workers** — moderate. A worker must be able to understand, without knowing the system's internals, what happened to their submission.
+- **Export readiness** — minor. How often a visit is blocked from the CSV by pending review.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-visit revisions
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries the complete visit payload and names the base revision it was authored against. A submission based on the current revision becomes the new current revision. A submission based on any other revision is stored whole as a competing revision, and the visit is flagged as having unresolved review work. A coordinator resolves by adopting one stored revision — current or competing — or by authoring a corrected payload combining what they judge correct. Every stored revision is retained verbatim with its author and receipt time.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No silent discard: **passes** — nothing is combined or dropped without a human decision.
+- Two-coordinator operability: **passes** — review work is a queue of whole visits; resolving needs no merge-rule knowledge.
+- Safety of related edits: **strong** — the server never decides that two edits are unrelated; every concurrent edit, however trivial, reaches a person.
+- Review load: **weak** — even edits to unrelated fields (an observer-name fix and a missing species) each create competing-revision review work.
+- Implementation burden: **strong** — the mechanism is revision bookkeeping; no field-level merge code to write or maintain.
+- Explainability: **strong** — "your edit was saved and is waiting for review against a newer version" is one sentence.
+- Export readiness: **weak** — a visit with any pending competing revision stays out of the CSV.
+
+**Summary.** Trades review volume for human judgment on every concurrent edit.
+
+### Field-aware three-way merge
+
+**Description.** Each submission carries a base snapshot and structured field-level changes. The server applies three-way merge rules: changes to disjoint fields combine automatically into a new current state; overlapping changes become review work. Automatic combination requires merge rules per field type — scalars, observer lists, species entries — maintained as the payload schema evolves. Review work consists of field-level conflicts rather than whole visits, and the export requires field-level provenance to stay inspectable.
+
+**Analysis.**
+- No silent discard: **passes** — every submitted change is either applied or reviewed; nothing is dropped.
+- Two-coordinator operability: **passes** — the queue is smaller, though diagnosing an odd merged state can require understanding the merge rules.
+- Safety of related edits: **weak** — merge rules keyed on field names can automatically combine scientifically related edits — a species-identification correction and the cover estimate written against the old identification — with no human ever seeing the pairing.
+- Review load: **strong** — disjoint edits never enter the queue; only true overlaps do.
+- Implementation burden: **weak** — merge rules must be written per field type, revisited whenever the visit schema changes, and tested against scientific relatedness for each combination.
+- Explainability: **partial** — "the server merged your edit with another" is simple to say but hard to show; proving which fields survived requires field-level provenance in every screen and export column.
+- Export readiness: **strong** — more visits are unblocked sooner.
+
+**Summary.** Trades implementation effort and safety risk for a smaller review queue and faster export readiness.
+
+### Rejected outright by the gate
+
+Last-write-wins (arrival order overwrites) and reject-stale (the server refuses stale-base submissions) both discard a worker's submitted observations without review. They fail the no-silent-discard gate and are not scored.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions.** Both surviving options pass the gates; the decision turns on the two major criteria, and they split: field-aware merge wins review load, whole-visit revisions win safety of related edits. Safety wins because the cost of a wrong merge — a scientifically plausible record no human ever examined — is invisible when it happens and hard to detect at export, while the cost of a larger queue is visible, measurable, and staffed by the people qualified to judge it. The no-silent-discard and inspectable-path tenets turn on exactly this asymmetry. The implementation and explainability criteria reinforce the choice for a team with no dedicated engineer to own merge rules.
+
+This is a lean, not a proof. Field-aware merge becomes credible again if measured review volume proves unmanageable for two coordinators and the team gains sustained engineering capacity to own merge rules — that is the re-run point for this decision.
+
+Committing to whole-visit revisions obligates:
+
+- **Tablet-sync**: retain each local visit's base revision ID across restarts and resubmission retries; submit whole-visit payloads naming that base; distinguish accepted-as-current from accepted-as-competing in the worker-facing response; warn workers when a visit they hold is under review, since any stale-based resubmission becomes competing review work rather than replacing newer data.
+- **Review-export**: a whole-payload revision comparison screen; export eligibility that excludes visits with unresolved review work; a resolution interface supporting both adopting a stored revision and authoring a corrected payload; contributor attribution (worker IDs, receipt times, coordinator, contributing revision IDs) so every exported value traces to a submission or a coordinator's resolution.
+- **Record store**: retain competing revisions and resolution metadata indefinitely, including the revision list and per-visit sequence numbers that make the comparison screen and attribution possible.
